# Style Showcase

This page deliberately uses **every** block and inline construct the markengine
supports, and combines them, so the shell's styling is proven against the real
engine output.

## Headings

# Heading level 1
## Heading level 2
### Heading level 3
#### Heading level 4
##### Heading level 5
###### Heading level 6

## Inline text styles

Plain text, then **bold**, *italic*, and ***bold italic together***. GFM
~~strikethrough~~ still works. In this engine `__double underscores__` mean
**underline**, not bold — a deliberate divergence from CommonMark. You can also
use the tag forms: [b]bold[/b], [i]italic[/i], [u]underline[/u], [s]strike[/s],
and [mark]a marked highlight[/mark]. The `==equals==` form ==also highlights==.

Inline `code` sits mid-sentence, and the rich form [code]keeps **nested** styles[/code].
Colour is available too: [color=#cc785c]terracotta text[/color] and
[bg=#f5e6df]a warm highlight background[/bg]. Styles nest —
**bold with [color=#b5532e]coloured italics *inside*[/color]**.

A hard line break comes from two trailing spaces:
…and this sentence sits on the next line.

Emoji shortcodes render inline: :warning: :check: :smile: :wave:

## Links, footnotes, mentions

A [regular link](https://example.com), a [titled link](https://example.com "Hover me"),
and a claim that needs a source.[^src] Internal page mentions look like
[[page:getting-started]] — with no resolver they fall back to the raw id, which is
itself a useful edge case to see.

[^src]: This is the footnote definition. Footnotes collect at the point they are defined.

## Blockquote

> A blockquote holds **formatted** text and can span multiple lines.
> It is the base of the callout and toggle constructs below.

## Callouts

Callouts are `> [!kind] title` blocks. The engine passes the *kind* straight
through as a class, so the shell can theme any of them:

> [!note] Note
> Neutral information the reader should not miss.

> [!tip] Tip
> A shortcut or better way to do something.

> [!info] Info
> Context that supports the surrounding text.

> [!warning] Warning
> Proceed carefully — this can bite.

> [!error] Error
> Something is wrong and needs fixing.

> [!success] Success
> The happy path completed.

> [!example] Example
> A worked illustration.

> [!faq] FAQ
> A question people actually ask.

> [!todo] Todo
> Still to be done.

## Toggles

> [toggle] Click to expand this toggle
> Hidden content lives here. It can hold **formatting**, `code`, and even lists:
> - first hidden point
> - second hidden point

## Lists

Unordered, with nesting and mixed markers:

- First level
  - Second level (indented)
    1. Ordered inside unordered
    2. Second ordered item
- Back to first level with `inline code`

Ordered, starting mid-sequence:

3. Third
4. Fourth
5. Fifth

Task list:

- [x] A completed task
- [ ] An open task with a [link](https://example.com)
- [x] Another done item :check:

## Table

Columns show left / centre / right alignment:

| Plane | Language | Owns | Latency budget |
|:------|:--------:|------|---------------:|
| Application | TypeScript | What users touch | 100 ms |
| Control | Go | Who can do what | 20 ms |
| Data | Rust | Moving bytes | 2 ms |

## Code block

```ts
export function renderDocs(source: string): string {
  const ast = parse(source);          // markdown → BlockNode[]
  return renderHtml(ast, {            // BlockNode[] → HTML
    classPrefix: "md",
    externalLinks: true,
  });
}
```

## Math

Inline math like $E = mc^2$ sits in a sentence. Block math stands alone:

$$
\int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}
$$

> [!note] Known ceiling
> Math is emitted as raw TeX. Rendering it to typeset math needs a client-side
> library (KaTeX/MathJax); the shell shows the source, styled, until then.

## Raw HTML and media

The engine now passes media tags straight through. Here an inline `<svg>` renders
self-contained — no external asset:

<figure class="xmd-figure">
<svg viewBox="0 0 320 120" width="320" height="120" role="img" aria-label="Three warm bars">
<rect x="16" y="70" width="80" height="40" rx="6" fill="#cc785c"></rect>
<rect x="120" y="40" width="80" height="70" rx="6" fill="#d89a80"></rect>
<rect x="224" y="20" width="80" height="90" rx="6" fill="#b5532e"></rect>
</svg>
<figcaption>An inline SVG, passed through untouched as a raw HTML block.</figcaption>
</figure>

Bare `<video>` and `<audio>` tags also survive parsing (they render empty players
here because no asset is attached — real assets arrive with the screenshots):

<video controls width="100%" style="max-width:480px;border-radius:10px"></video>

